   "text": "<answer>\n## 1. Diagnosis\n\n1. **Confirm the migration mismatch**\n   - Inspect `0002_initial.py` and `00015_add_index.py` for duplicate `CreateModel` operations.\n   - Check whether the model name, app label, and table name are identical in both migrations.\n   - Review recent commits for accidental duplicate model definitions or migration edits.\n\n2. **Inspect migration state**\n   - Run:\n     ```bash\n     python manage.py showmigrations <app>\n     ```\n   - Check whether `0015_add_index` is marked applied.\n   - Inspect the database migration table, for example:\n     ```sql\n     SELECT app, name, applied\n     FROM django_migrations\n     WHERE app = '<app>';\n     ```\n\n3. **Inspect the actual database schema**\n   - Confirm that `orders_order` exists.\n   - Compare its columns, constraints, and indexes with the schema expected by the migrations.\n   - Check whether the table contains data that must be preserved.\n\n4. **Compare generated SQL**\n   - Run:\n     ```bash\n     python manage.py sqlmigrate <app> 0002\n     python manage.py sqlmigrate <app> 0015\n     ```\n   - Determine whether both migrations attempt to create the same table.\n\n5. **Check deployment history**\n   - Determine whether the table was created by:\n     - `0002_initial.py`\n     - a manual database operation\n     - an earlier failed deployment\n     - a different environment or database\n\n---\n\n## 2. Fix Options\n\n### Option A: Correct the migration graph\n\nUse this when the table created by `0002_initial.py` is correct and `0015_add_index.py` should only add indexes or other objects.\n\n1. Remove the duplicate `CreateModel(\"Order\")` operation from `0015_add_index.py`.\n2. Keep only the intended operations, such as:\n   - `AddIndex`\n   - `AddConstraint`\n   - `CreateIndex`\n3. If `0015_add_index.py` has not been applied, edit it before running migrations.\n4. If it has already been applied, do not edit it in place. Create a new corrective migration that reconciles the schema.\n5. Run:\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run\n   python manage.py migrate\n   ```\n\n### Option B: Add a reconciliation migration\n\nUse this when the existing table is authoritative and must be preserved.\n\n1. Create a new migration after `0015_add_index.py`.\n2. Add operations that ensure the required table, columns, constraints, and indexes exist.\n3. If there is an accidental duplicate table, drop only the duplicate table after confirming it contains no required data.\n4. Avoid using `--fake` unless the database schema exactly matches the migration state.\n5. Run:\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py migrate\n   ```\n\n### Option C: Rebuild from a clean baseline\n\nUse this only when the table is accidental and contains no data.\n\n1. Back up the database.\n2. Remove or correct the duplicate migration operations.\n3. Drop the incorrect table in a controlled environment.\n4. Reset migration state only after confirming the database is disposable.\n5. Apply the corrected migration history from the beginning.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verification\n\n1. **Check migration consistency**\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run\n   python manage.py migrate --plan\n   ```\n\n2. **Apply migrations**\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py migrate\n   ```\n\n3. **Confirm migration status**\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py showmigrations <app>\n   ```\n\n4. **Verify the database schema**\n   - Confirm `orders_order` exists exactly once.\n   - Verify all expected columns, constraints, and indexes.\n   - Confirm no unintended tables remain.\n\n5. **Run application tests**\n   ```bash\n   python manage.py test\n   ```\n\n6. **Perform smoke tests**\n   - Create, retrieve, update, and delete an `Order`.\n   - Verify queries using the newly added indexes.\n   - Confirm existing order data remains intact.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Rollback Plan\n\n1. **Before making changes**\n   - Take a database backup or snapshot.\n   - Commit the corrected migration files separately.\n\n2. **If the corrected migration has not been applied**\n   - Revert the migration-file changes.\n   - Run the last known-good migration state.\n\n3. **If the corrected migration has been applied**\n   - Do not delete the applied migration.\n   - Create a reverse migration that removes the corrective changes.\n   - Alternatively, restore the database backup.\n\n4. **If a destructive reset was performed**\n   - Restore the database backup.\n   - Reapply the known-good migration history.\n   - Verify data integrity before reopening the application.\n\n5. **After rollback**\n   - Run migration checks, tests, and schema verification again.\n   - Confirm the application and database migration states are synchronized.\n</answer>",
