  "7": {
   "text": "<answer>\nStep 2 failed because `pip` attempted to install `certifi` into the system Python directory:\n\n`/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/certifi`\n\nThe current user does not have permission to modify that location. This usually happens when installing into the system Python instead of an isolated virtual environment.\n\nRecovery commands, run from the repository root:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\nRevised plan from step 2 onward:\n\n2. Install dependencies in a virtual environment:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install --upgrade pip\npython -m pip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n3. Run tests:\n\n```bash\npython -m pytest tests/\n```\n\n4. Deploy:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t app .\n```\n\nImpact:\n\n- Step 3 is temporarily affected because dependencies were not installed, but it should work after the virtual environment setup.\n- Step 4 is not directly affected because Docker builds independently of the host virtual environment. However, if the Dockerfile installs Python dependencies system-wide, it could encounter a similar permission issue inside the image.\n</answer>",
