  "13": {
   "text": "<answer>\n# ADR-001: Dedicated Notification Service\n\n**Status:** Proposed  \n**Decision:** Adopt the hybrid option: retain the Django monolith and introduce a dedicated notification service.\n\n## Context\n\nThe system must support:\n\n- 10M registered users and approximately 100K concurrent users\n- Email, push, in-app, and SMS delivery\n- In-app notification latency below 500 ms\n- Existing AWS infrastructure and the current team of 3 backend engineers and 1 DevOps engineer\n- A current Django monolith with Celery workers\n\nThe architecture must balance delivery reliability, latency, operational complexity, and team capacity.\n\n## Options Considered\n\n| Option | Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Keep the monolith and add Redis Pub/Sub** | Fastest to implement; low latency; familiar technology; minimal architectural change | Redis Pub/Sub is not durable by itself; failures can lose notifications; monolith and notification workload remain coupled; channel-specific retries and backpressure are difficult; shared resources may threaten the 500 ms in-app SLO |\n| **2. Migrate to microservices and Kafka** | Strong decoupling; durable event streaming; independent scaling; good for complex event processing | Large migration effort; Kafka operations and monitoring add significant DevOps burden; higher infrastructure and engineering cost; unnecessary complexity for the current team and requirements |\n| **3. Keep the monolith and add a dedicated notification service** | Preserves the stable monolith; isolates notification scaling and failure domains; supports durable queues, retries, and channel-specific policies; enables incremental rollout; fits the existing AWS footprint and team size | Introduces a service boundary, event/API contract, distributed observability, and eventual consistency between request acceptance and final delivery |\n\n## Decision\n\nAdopt **Option 3: Hybrid architecture**.\n\nThe monolith will remain responsible for user data, application state, and business workflows. It will publish a normalized `NotificationRequested` event after the relevant transaction commits. A separate notification service will consume that event and own:\n\n- Notification persistence and deduplication\n- Channel selection and prioritization\n- In-app delivery through the existing in-app transport\n- Email, push, and SMS provider integration\n- Retries, rate limiting, circuit breakers, and dead-letter handling\n- Delivery status, preferences, and suppression rules\n\nThe in-app path should be optimized independently from slower provider-based channels. The service should acknowledge successful acceptance quickly and use the existing AWS durable messaging capability for asynchronous delivery and recovery.\n\n## Rationale\n\n1. **Meets the latency requirement:** A dedicated in-app path avoids blocking on email, SMS, or push providers and can be load-tested independently against the 500 ms target.\n2. **Improves reliability:** Durable queuing, idempotent processing, retries, and dead-letter handling provide stronger delivery guarantees than Redis Pub/Sub alone.\n3. **Controls operational risk:** The team avoids a full microservices migration and does not introduce Kafka\u2019s additional operational burden.\n4. **Fits team capacity:** The change is incremental and can be implemented by the existing backend and DevOps team.\n5. **Preserves investment:** Existing Django code, AWS infrastructure, and Celery capabilities remain useful during the transition.\n6. **Allows future evolution:** Notification-specific scaling, provider changes, and analytics can evolve without changing the monolith.\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Positive\n\n- The monolith can be migrated channel by channel without a big-bang rewrite.\n- Notification failures are isolated from core application availability.\n- In-app notifications can receive a separate SLO and capacity allocation.\n- Provider outages can be contained with retries and circuit breakers.\n- User notification preferences and delivery history can become a dedicated domain.\n\n### Negative\n\n- The system gains a distributed boundary and requires explicit event versioning.\n- Delivery may become eventually consistent; the monolith should not assume immediate provider delivery.\n- Additional monitoring, tracing, alerting, and deployment procedures are required.\n- The team must define ownership for the new service and its AWS resources.\n- Initial implementation requires more design and testing than simply adding Redis.\n\n## Implementation Phases\n\n1. Define the `NotificationRequested` event schema, idempotency key, and delivery-status model.\n2. Build the notification service with durable queue consumption, deduplication, and basic in-app delivery.\n3. Add email, push, and SMS providers with retries, rate limits, and dead-letter queues.\n4. Migrate producers incrementally while retaining the existing Celery path as a fallback.\n5. Load-test at 100K concurrent users and verify the in-app latency SLO under failure conditions.\n6. Reassess Kafka or further service decomposition only if measured throughput or domain complexity justifies it.\n\n## Final Recommendation\n\nChoose the **hybrid architecture**. It provides the best balance of low-latency in-app delivery, reliable asynchronous processing, operational simplicity, and incremental migration. Avoid Redis Pub/Sub as the sole durability mechanism, and defer Kafka until the system has a demonstrated need that the dedicated service cannot satisfy.\n</answer>",
